      "lem:arm-tape-equivalence",
      "thm:minimax-length-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "This separates the one-sided condition needed for a policy-uniform upper bound from the two-sided comparability regime needed to identify an exact minimax sampling frontier.",
    "gap": "Shi, Wang, and Wu use one-sided forced exploration for delayed asymptotic inference under outcome-delay independence; the proposition isolates what survives under unrestricted outcome-delay dependence without attributing the new two-sided regime to them.",
    "consumer": "Analysts whose logs certify only a forced-exploration floor can retain the honest construction while reporting an upper guarantee rather than an exact policy-class minimax rate.",
    "proof_tex": "The proof of lem:quota-success uses only ass:one-sided-forced-exploration for arm \\(1\\) and ass:control-floor for arm \\(0\\); therefore its deterministic quota guarantee holds uniformly on \\(\\mathfrak M_T^{\\mathrm{low}}\\).  Lem:arm-tape-equivalence and lem:old-cohort-correction also hold on that class.  The concentration, gate, and studentization arguments in thm:uniform-gated-inference use the policy only through those deterministic quotas.  They therefore give\n\\[\n \\inf_{(P,\\Pi,T)\\in\\mathfrak M_T^{\\mathrm{low}}}\n P\\{\\tau\\in I_{T,\\Delta}\\}\\geq1-\\eta-o(1).\n\\]\nThe branchwise length calculation in the proof of thm:minimax-length-frontier likewise uses only the quota orders and the delay envelope, and hence gives\n\\[\n \\sup_{(P,\\Pi,T)\\in\\mathfrak M_T^{\\mathrm{low}}}\n E_{P,\\Pi}|I_{T,\\Delta}|\\leq C_\\eta r_{T,\\Delta}.\n\\]\nThe lower allocation envelope alone does not bound the actual number of designated-arm assignments from above.  Thus it cannot yield the sampling-information obstruction used in the converse, which is why no matching sampling lower bound is concluded on this enlarged class.",
